{"kind":"assert","text":"Builder: the I3 implementation plan is written at 5b3e42bad on request/i3-authority-effects (notes/2026-10-05-i3-implementation-plan.md, one file, no source yet). The branch stands on the I2 second head 50bba45c, which is under review with changes requested (dce192f4) and is not accepted; I rebase when I2 moves.\n\nMain finding. A full run from a real founding to a real merge cannot be made yet. It needs four rulings from the scope contract's owner, which is my own revision 13 follow-through (c75205df): how a lane knows its membership (P20), an act entry that retains a second observation (P19), bytes beside an intent that are not a text (P21), and the address of a notice from a lane (P23). Revision 13 as filed adopts P20 and P23 and leaves P19 and P21 open as R1-53 and R1-54. Everything else can be built and shown.\n\nScope. Of 32 elements of the authority note's sections 11 to 11.2: 23 can be built and tested whole now, in process, on the adopted texts; 7 in part (five wait on the contract, one on the authority note's successor for what makes a hold have a workspace, one on capacity cc570904); 2 can be built but need a separate commission for evidence on a real runner. Of the 25 validator gaps: 8 are in the source now, 3 are agreed forms I3 builds, 9 are held as platform code (no new member of any entry or message; a pure function in the commit; replay derives it again or answers unsupported-definition), 3 are split with a blocked part, 1 is blocked whole.\n\nSize. I3 as commissioned is too large for one review: about three times I2, and mostly judgments, not tables. The plan proposes five milestones, each reviewable and landable: M1 authority (founding, membership, observation and grant guard, sessions, enrolment, revocation, recovery, incidents); M2 capabilities (hold and git-read records, staging, pins, ancestry, tokens, gateway; the scripted capability is removed and I2's waiting rows W1 run); M3 checks; M4 publication (reservation, compare-and-set, readback, receipt; A6 and A7 in both orders with the lost push reply); M5 assembly (lanes on the real repository, last removals, guides, measurement; the full I3 is filed here). 35 steps; 49 named tests, each one invariant with its exact command. Estimate, labelled as one: 55 to 75 tests added and a warm gate under 25 seconds; I2's estimate was low by more than two times and the same is likely here.\n\nQuestions for the planner.\nQ9. May the milestones land one by one while bcf5ec17 stays open, as I2's base does? The plan assumes yes.\nQ8. The checker job on a real runner and the session with a real Git host need separate commissions, as you said. Until then those two elements are shown in process only and labelled.\nQ6, for hugh as operator: which Git host does the destination publish to? The earlier code used a Cloudflare Git service, not GitHub. The plan keeps the host behind one interface and proves nothing about a real host until that session.\nQ5. Who builds capacity in items, bytes and pending requests: I3, or the capacity follow-through cc570904 first?\n\nTwo real faults found in the parked code that I3 must not carry over, both named by the commission's review demand: the earlier branch push checks nothing about the commit it sends, and the runner never reads a commit's parents.\n\nI am starting the two small base steps now. The steps that reshape the authority, definition and capability ports wait until the I2 repairs are in, so that they do not collide."}